Thanks so much for the tip. This function worked well! I am noticing one small issue in that arrivals are able to sort of sneak into the next resource in front of another arrival that has been waiting longer. I was wondering if anyone else had this problem and how it could be corrected? I already have my arrivals prioritized based on the time it starts, by adding in this small function to the trajectory prior to calling "interleaved_resources", so that the arrivals with earliest start times have highest priority, and I am not allowing preemption on the resources:

  set_prioritization(values =function(){
    prio<-10-env %>% now()
    c(prio, prio, FALSE)
  }
  ) %>%

I did a very simple example in which there were only resources A & B, each with cap of 2 and B with queue lim of 1 and the delay/timeout was 2 & 3 for A&B, respectively. The arrivals started every .5 and I added in a function to record the time that a timeout is over, in order to understand how much time is spent waiting to seize resource B and found at least one instance in which an arrival6 that finished its timeout at t=8, after arrival5(finished its timeout at 7 and then had to wait until 8.5 to enter resource B) was able to proceed to the next resource before the prior arrival, at t=8.

Could this be due to the way that the "wait_until" function handles priorities?

