El jue., 7 jun. 2018 a las 15:53, Bridget (<bbas...@gmail.com>) escribió:
>
> Thanks so much for the tip. This function worked well! I am noticing one small issue in that arrivals are able to sort of sneak into the next resource in front of another arrival that has been waiting longer. I was wondering if anyone else had this problem and how it could be corrected? I already have my arrivals prioritized based on the time it starts, by adding in this small function to the trajectory prior to calling "interleaved_resources", so that the arrivals with earliest start times have highest priority, and I am not allowing preemption on the resources:
>
>   set_prioritization(values =function(){
>     prio<-10-env %>% now()
>     c(prio, prio, FALSE)
>   }
>   ) %>%
>
> I did a very simple example in which there were only resources A & B, each with cap of 2 and B with queue lim of 1 and the delay/timeout was 2 & 3 for A&B, respectively. The arrivals started every .5 and I added in a function to record the time that a timeout is over, in order to understand how much time is spent waiting to seize resource B and found at least one instance in which an arrival6 that finished its timeout at t=8, after arrival5(finished its timeout at 7 and then had to wait until 8.5 to enter resource B) was able to proceed to the next resource before the prior arrival, at t=8.
>
> Could this be due to the way that the "wait_until" function handles priorities?

wait_until translates into trap > wait > untrap, and does not handle
priorities at all. Entities arriving there follow a simple FIFO order
when a signal is received. The problem (the first one and this second
one when you add priorities) has to do with simultaneous events.
simmer does its best to try to get them right, but if you have so many
times happening at the same time (in this case, a release from A + a
release from B + arrivals waiting), there's no way to cover all
possible cases.

If your final simulation has random delays and generation patterns (or
at least they are not integers or multiples), your problems will most
probably disappear, and you won't even need the prioritization
function. Nonetheless, I would try this one simpler approach (note
that queue_size=Inf for the "B token" this time):

library(simmer)

interleaved_resources <- function(.trj, names, delays) {
  for (i in seq_along(names)) {
    .trj %>%
      seize(names[i]) %>%
      timeout(delays[i])
    if (i < length(names)) .trj %>%

      seize(paste(names[i+1], "token"))

    .trj %>%
      release(names[i])
    if (i > 1) .trj %>%
      release(paste(names[i], "token"))
  }
  .trj
}

t <- trajectory() %>%

  interleaved_resources(c("A", "B"), c(2, 3))

simmer() %>%
  add_resource("A", 2, Inf) %>%
  add_resource("B", 2, 1) %>%
  add_resource("B token", 3, Inf) %>%
  add_generator("dummy", t, function() 0.5) %>%
  run(20) %>%
  get_mon_arrivals()

Please, let me know if this works.

Iñaki

